></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"ZmylsCq03N"},{"type":"text","value":" (the means are equal). Either way, conceptually, H~o~ claims that there is no relationship between the two relevant variables.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"IRmt5iVN0q"}],"key":"YpJuk5h2iR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first way of writing the hypotheses (using a difference between the means) will be easier to use when (in the future) we look for a difference that is not 0.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"i4fhYIg7mi"}],"key":"RPk1mhaNMR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each one of the three alternatives claims that there is a difference between the means. The two one-sided alternatives specify the nature of the difference; either negative, indicating that μ~1~ is smaller than μ~2~, or positive, indicating that μ~1~ is larger than μ~2~. The two-sided alternative, as usual, is more general and simply claims that a difference exists. As before, it should be clear from the context of the problem which of the three alternatives is appropriate.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"GCydOi7pMN"}],"key":"Bxl1imDeIY"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comment","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"Qha1adVoRK"}],"identifier":"comment","label":"Comment","html_id":"comment","implicit":true,"key":"fEqkieG0HZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that our parameter of interest in this case (the parameter about which we a